{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a count of Tripoli who ruled from 1146 to 1193. He is a significant figure in the history of the Crusades and the Levant. His reign marked a period of both prosperity and conflict for the county.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1105 in Tripoli. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Countess Eva of Montferrat. He inherited the county upon the death of his father in 1146. His ascension to the countship was not without challenges as he faced opposition from various factions within the county including rival families and disgruntled nobles. He quickly consolidated his power through strategic alliances and military campaigns.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw a flourishing of Tripoli's economy. The city became a major trading center connecting Europe with the East. Tripoli prospered from its control of vital trade routes facilitating the exchange of goods such as spices textiles and silk. The county also maintained a significant military presence defending its territories from Muslim incursions and rival European powers.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*\n\nRaymond III's rule was characterized by numerous military campaigns and conflicts. He engaged in several wars against the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim powers seeking to protect Tripoli's independence. He also fought against the Normans who sought to expand their influence in the Levant.  His military successes helped to maintain Tripoli's autonomy and its position as a regional power. He also participated in Crusader campaigns although his role was not always prominent. He was known for his strong leadership and his ability to rally his troops.\n\nThe county also faced internal conflicts.  There were power struggles between different families and factions vying for control. Raymond III managed to maintain stability through a combination of diplomacy and military force. He often relied on alliances and strategic marriages to solidify his position.\n\n*Legacy and Decline*\n\nRaymond III's reign is often viewed as a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli. He successfully defended the county's independence for a significant period and fostered economic growth. However his reign was not without its flaws.  The county faced increasing pressure from both Muslim and European powers.  \n\nBy the end of his reign Tripoli was weakened and vulnerable.  The county lost territory to the Crusaders and the Muslims.  Raymond III died in 1193 leaving behind a legacy of both achievement and decline. His efforts to maintain Tripoli's independence ultimately proved insufficient in the face of the growing power of other entities in the region.  He is remembered as a valiant ruler who defended his people and his land against overwhelming odds.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1434636116027832}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -6.854299135738984e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.0874934121966362}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.029306435957551003}, {"token": "▁count", "logprob": -2.7451179027557373}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -0.07169874012470245}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": -0.011469733901321888}, {"token": "▁who", "logprob": -0.17361369729042053}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.25805822014808655}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.004177534487098455}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.003325172932818532}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00018892886873800308}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0022743092849850655}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.7879870533943176}, {"token": "6", "logprob": -0.13841238617897034}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.06253340095281601}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.0005150898941792548}, {"token": "1", "logprob